# Non-negotiable architecture and security rules

## Tenant isolation

- Every tenant-owned table must have forced row-level security.
- Every business query must execute inside a transaction-local tenant context.
- Tenant identity must come only from verified authentication and membership.
- Never accept an organization ID from the browser as authorization.
- Verify object ownership at every boundary.
- Add tests proving that one organization cannot read, change, start, end, or bill another organization’s resources.
- Keep the runtime database role restricted and unable to assume the migration role.
- Security-definer functions must have an explicit safe search path, minimal privileges, validated inputs, and narrowly scoped execute grants.

## Authentication and authorization

- Audit Clerk JWT verification using the current official Clerk SDK.
- Validate authorized parties/audience/issuer as appropriate.
- Replace deprecated Clerk route-matcher authentication with the supported Next.js 16/Clerk approach.
- Implement clear roles: owner, admin, member, viewer.
- Define and enforce a permissions matrix for every API operation.
- Protect onboarding from duplicate-organization races.
- Add secure member invitation and role-management flows if required by the approved handoff.
- Handle expired sessions and deactivated members consistently.

## Input validation

- Use shared schemas for API payloads.
- Reject unknown fields where appropriate.
- Bound all names, instructions, messages, files, metadata, transcripts, and provider payloads.
- Normalize phone numbers to E.164.
- Never trust provider callbacks without signature verification.
- Prevent mass-assignment of tenant, role, balance, status, pricing, and provider fields.

## Money

- Represent money as integer paise or PostgreSQL bigint.
- Never use floating-point arithmetic for balances or charges.
- Never credit a wallet from a browser redirect or unverified client response.
- Only verified, idempotent Razorpay events may credit funds.
- Never start a billable call without an atomic reservation.
- Prevent concurrent calls from reserving the same available funds.
- Use immutable ledger entries and compensating entries rather than history mutation.
- Snapshot the rate and all pricing inputs before the call.
- Define rounding explicitly and consistently.
- Every payment, reservation, settlement, release, refund, and correction needs a stable idempotency key and audit metadata.
- Duplicate provider events must be harmless.

## Voice sessions

- Keep the complete streaming pipeline.
- Ensure correct audio sample rates, channel counts, frame sizes, resampling, bounded queues, and backpressure.
- Cancel obsolete LLM/TTS work when the caller interrupts.
- Clear queued playback and prevent stale audio from resuming.
- Clean up rooms and provider streams on disconnect, timeout, shutdown, navigation, and cancellation races.
- Provide useful errors for unavailable workers, STT, LLM, TTS, LiveKit, and telephony.
- Do not log raw audio, credentials, JWTs, or sensitive transcript content by default.
- Verify whether the current worker produces event-loop blocking warnings and move blocking work away from the real-time loop.
- Preserve the standalone prototype as a diagnostic tool, but make the dashboard workflow the supported product experience.

